00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:31,080
 So the first function we'll be creating is a function that doubles a number.

7
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:34,800
 So we'll call it double, and it's going to take a single parameter,

8
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:39,120
 and it's going to be an integer parameter, and it's going to also return an integer.

9
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:42,600
 We're just going to return the number plus itself, that way we can double it.

10
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:48,800
 We'll then create a second function name add, and that we're going to be adding two numbers together.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:53,200
 So we have a left hand side, a right hand side, and both are going to be integers.

12
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:57,600
 Since we're using the same type integer for both left hand side and right hand side,

13
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,480
 we don't need to specify it two times.

14
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:03,920
 So we can just use a comma, and use int once, and both of these here will be integers.

15
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:11,640
 The add function is going to be returning an integer, so let's return left hand side plus right hand side.

16
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:17,400
 Next, we'll make a function called greet, and that's going to display a message.

17
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:23,040
 This function has no input parameters and no output data, so we can just use parentheses with nothing in it.

18
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:26,400
 And we'll just use a blank space right there.

19
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:31,440
 In our greet function, we'll use the format package, and we'll do a print line,

20
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,520
 hello from greet function.

21
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:38,400
 We go down on our main function, we can start utilizing these functions we just created.

22
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,720
 So the first function we'll use is the greet function.

23
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:47,280
 To call the function, all we need to do is type the name of the function and our two parentheses.

24
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:54,000
 The next function we'll use is the double function. So I'm going to create and assign a variable name dozen,

25
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,440
 and we're going to take the double function, we'll double the number six,

26
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:58,960
 and that'll give us 12, which is a dozen.

27
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:04,080
 We'll need to print out this result, that way we can see that we get the correct number.

28
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:15,360
 Next up, we'll create a baker's dozen, so a baker's dozen is 13.

29
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,240
 So we'll use the add function, and we're going to add our dozen that we already have,

30
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:23,200
 we'll add one to it, and we'll print that out as well.

31
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:35,440
 So what we're doing here with the add function is we're adding the value of dozen and one.

32
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:41,120
 Here in our double function, we're doubling six, which is 12, that'll get placed into dozen.

33
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,720
 But we're then going to add the dozen from here, that's going to be 12,

34
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:52,320
 and we'll add one to it, and this is going to place 13 into a baker's dozen.

35
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:54,080
 So baker's dozen will have 13.

36
00:02:54,080 --> 00:03:00,640
 And finally, we're going to have both functions call at the same time, so we'll do

37
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:07,200
 another baker's dozen, and that will be the result of add and double,

38
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,320
 and then we'll print that out.

